Explanation:

✅First: Notice the change detection startegy in the Race component is still Eager. We could change
    it into OnPush but for the sake of clarity, i decided not to change it.
    Eager or OnPush, it doesn't matter, it doesn't change the log because 🧠A COMPONENT'S CHANGE DETECTION
    STRATEGY ONLY DETERMINES WHETHER ANGULAR WILL CHECK THAT COMPONENT'S OWN VIEW

    In simple words, It doesn't matter whether Race is Eager or OnPush in this example because the click
    event originates inside Race. Angular automatically marks Race as dirty, so it will always be
    checked.


⁉️ Why don't we get the logs from Pony and Image components?

    Well first of all, 🔥🧠The key idea behind OnPush is that Angular doesn't check a component on every
    change detection cycle. Instead, it only checks the when angular has a reason to believe something
    affecting that component may have changed.
    The evidence depends on the trigger:
        1️⃣A new input reference:
            -> Angular stores the previous input value.
            -> During a parent's check, it compares the old and new values using ===.
            -> If they are different, Angular has evidence that the child's input changed, so it marks the
                child for checking.
        
        2️⃣An event in the component's subtree:
            -> Angular itself handled the event (e.g. (click), (input)).
            -> Since the event originated inside that subtree, Angular has evidence that code there may
                have modified state.
            -> So it checks that subtree, even if the components use OnPush.

        3️⃣A signal changes:
            -> When the template reads a signal (e.g. count()), Angular records that dependency.
            -> Later, when the signal's value changes, the signal notifies Angular.
            -> That notification is the evidence that the component needs checking.
        
        4️⃣An async pipe receives a new value:
            -> The async pipe subscribes to the observable.
            -> When the observable emits, the async pipe tells Angular to mark the component for checking.
            -> The emission is the evidence.

        5️⃣markForCheck() or detectChanges():
            -> You're explicitly telling Angular, "This component may have changed."
            -> Angular trusts you and performs the check.
    
